          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %strong Algebra
            is the manipulation of symbols without (necessarily) regard for their meaning, especially in a way that can be formalized in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+logic"} cartesian logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} dual
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/number+theory"} number theory
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
          %p The word ‘algebra’ is often also used for an algebraic structure:
          %ul
            %li
              often by default an
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+unital+algebra"} associative unital algebra
            %li
              more generally a
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+object"} monoid object
            %li
              more generally in a different way, a
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonassociative+algebra"} nonassociative algebra
            %li
              an
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+an+operad"} algebra over an operad
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/PROP"} PROP
            %li
              an
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+for+an+endofunctor"} algebra for an endofunctor
            %li
              a model of any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} algebraic theory
              or anything studied in
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+algebra"} universal algebra
            %li
              higher categorical analogues, many object/family versions of algebras, for example
              = succeed "s," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebroid"} algebroid
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudoalgebra"} pseudoalgebra
              = succeed "s)" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-algebra"} 2-algebra
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudomonad"} pseudomonad
              = succeed "s)." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-monad"} 2-monad
          %p
            Various fields of mathematics or mathematical concepts can be manipulated in an algebraic or symbolic way, and such approaches or formalized subfields have names like
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+algebra"} categorical algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+algebra"} homotopical algebra
            and so on. Methods of combinatorics which involve much algebra, and manipulations with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            in particular, are called
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                algebraic combinatorics
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/algebraic+combinatorics"} ?
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %p
